Yeah. I just got an answer today...-taso by default will limit a process to    
~255 megs. Reading in the malloc man page, it talks about how to override 
this. I don't have the notes here on hand but there was two ways around 
this.
(note the flag may be a little off as I am doing this from memory, I'll 
post a clear follow up tomorrow when I get back to the culprit computers)

At link time, if I linked with just -taso, I get the 255 meg limitation. 
However if I link with -taso -non_share. The program then works like a 
charm above the 255 meg limitation.

I couldn't use the linker -non_share as it had issues with the way we link 
our apps. However, there is another way around this(all sorts of good info 
on this in the malloc man page).

There is a global var in malloc that is __sbrk_override. By default if set 
to 0, you get the limitation. If you set it to 1 in your program, once 
malloc fails at the 255 limit, it begins to allocation "past the shared 
libraries" It works for me. The man page describes what it does better than 
I can.

-grant


