Ken,

You've identified one possible cause.  The other is the power supply itself,
which may be marginal, despite acceptable voltage levels.  Yet another unlikely
cause is a screwed up momentary on-off switch.

Try first running the system with the cover off, to reduce the odds of a thermal
problem.  While you're at it, check the CPU inside.  It may not have a cooling
fan at all.  Many DEC Pentium II systems do not, favoring a large ventilation
fan blowing across the processor heat sink.

Next, trace the wire leads from the on-off switch to the main logic board or
riser card (I forget which).  Disconnect the power lead, and carefully short out
the pair of pins to which the on-off swich leads were connected.  You'll know
quickly enough if the on-off switch is bad.

If you need either the small wire harness including on-off switch or the
ventilation fan, I have both here in my warehouse of older DEC boxes, being sold
for parts.  Let me know what you need... Ben Myers

